import ModellingLayout from '@/layouts/ModellingLayout.astro';
---

<ModellingLayout title="Bring evidence that others can interpret" description="A useful source comes with its context: where it came from, which version it is, what it demonstrates and how it may be used. Prepare that context before submitting a file." chapter="Contribute · Evidence" claim="guide">
  <section aria-labelledby="purpose">
    <h2 id="purpose">Explain what the material helps us understand</h2>
    <p>
      A blank form, field dictionary, published definition or fictional scenario can
      reveal a modelling issue. The important part is the connection to a question.
      “This report template distinguishes inspection date from issue date” is more
      useful than an unexplained folder of documents.
    </p>
    <p>
      Existing systems and standards contain valuable knowledge. They also reflect
      particular purposes, versions and implementation choices. A nested field does
      not automatically establish a distinct thing in the world, and a field's absence
      does not prove a concept is unnecessary. The source is evidence to interpret,
      not an instruction to reproduce its structure unchanged.
    </p>
  </section>

  <figure class="modelling-figure" aria-labelledby="envelope-caption">
    <figcaption id="envelope-caption">The evidence envelope: what should accompany a source</figcaption>
    <dl>
      <dt>Origin</dt><dd>Source organisation and the owner or steward able to explain the material.</dd>
      <dt>Version and time</dt><dd>Document version, date and the period for which its information applies.</dd>
      <dt>Purpose</dt><dd>The question it helps answer and the working-group context it concerns.</dd>
      <dt>Standing</dt><dd>Whether it is authoritative, informative, illustrative or disputed—and why.</dd>
      <dt>Restrictions</dt><dd>Permission to share, confidentiality, licence, reuse and retention conditions.</dd>
      <dt>Interpretation</dt><dd>The precise passage, field or example that matters, including any known limitations.</dd>
    </dl>
  </figure>

  <section aria-labelledby="case">
    <h2 id="case">Harbour Court: retain the difference between versions</h2>
    <p>
      Suppose the group is examining our fictional inspection-date problem. Report
      version 1 was issued on 14 August 2026; version 2 corrected it on 20 August.
      Both describe the inspection of 12 August. An evidence note should explain that
      relationship rather than supplying only a file called “latest-report”.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the question is why an earlier decision used a particular statement, the
      earlier version may matter. If the question is what the corrected report now
      says, the later version matters. The model should let reviewers distinguish
      those questions without treating the correction as another inspection.
    </p>
    <details>
      <summary>Hard case: two sources disagree and neither is clearly superseded</summary>
      <p>Keep both interpretations visible, with their source, version, context and any known limitations. Explain the disagreement and who needs to review it. “Newest wins” is not a general authority rule: a newer publication may describe a different subject, purpose or period. This guide does not decide which source is authoritative for a particular property claim.</p>
    </details>
    <p>This is a teaching illustration. No report files are supplied here, and it does not demonstrate evidence-processing coverage in the current candidate.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="prepare">
    <h2 id="prepare">Prepare the smallest useful, permitted submission</h2>
    <ol class="modelling-steps">
      <li><h3>Choose the question</h3><p>State the ambiguity, rule or exception. Identify the relevant model page or candidate if one exists.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Choose evidence you may share</h3><p>Use a permitted blank template, non-production specification or invented example. Do not assume that possession of a document gives permission to submit it.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Keep source context intact</h3><p>Preserve the document's identity and version. Explain any excerpt or transformation so a reviewer does not mistake it for the complete original.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Describe its limits</h3><p>An organisation's practice is not automatically an industry-wide rule. Say what the evidence establishes and what it does not.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Use the authorised intake</h3><p>Follow the current member guide for your group and organisation. Discussion channels and page comments are not substitutes for controlled file intake.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="safety">
    <h2 id="safety">Keep sensitive material out of the example</h2>
    <p>
      Do not submit personal, customer or live property-transaction data, credentials,
      secrets, or material restricted by contract, licence, confidentiality or NDA.
      Replacing a person's name may leave a case recognisable from its address or
      unusual circumstances. A fully invented example is often sufficient to expose
      the modelling issue.
    </p>
    <p>
      The current intake guidance also excludes executables, active scripts, macros,
      encrypted or password-protected files and archives. Audio and video are not
      accepted through that intake; use a permitted transcript with its date,
      speakers and context. These are current operational guidance, not ontology
      rules. Check the <a href="/development/working-groups/member-guide/source-material-and-sharepoint">source-material
      and SharePoint guide</a> for the complete requirements and permitted routes.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="intake">
    <h2 id="intake">What the website provides today</h2>
    <p>
      For an active group with the described arrangements, source intake uses a
      separate SharePoint site and authorised organisation folders. The member guide
      explains access and the README that accompanies submissions. This page does
      not upload files, change permissions or guarantee that a particular account
      has access.
    </p>
    <p>
      A submission is not automatically public or shared with all participants.
      Screening, provenance and human interpretation come before any resulting
      proposition. Being accepted into the intake does not make a document's contents
      correct, make them authoritative for every context or approve a model change.
    </p>
    <p><a href="/semantic-modelling/contribute/what-happens-next">Follow the question from evidence to review →</a></p>
  </section>
</ModellingLayout>
